diff --git a/README_MULTI_LIBRETRO.md b/README_MULTI_LIBRETRO.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e7079ed
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README_MULTI_LIBRETRO.md
@@ -0,0 +1,167 @@
+# mGBA Multi 0.3.0
+
+This is a deliberately small, linkless libretro frontend built directly on
+upstream mGBA. It runs one selected GBA ROM in one, two, or three independent
+instances.
+
+## Baseline contract
+
+- Controller ports 1, 2, and 3 always control Players 1, 2, and 3.
+- Screen position, controller port, execution lane, and save slice use the same
+  permanent player index.
+- One instance is `240x160`; two are `480x160` side by side; three are
+  `480x320`, with P1/P2 above and P3 centered below. RetroArch scales this
+  native RGB565 composite without changing any screen's 3:2 aspect ratio.
+- The ordinary RetroArch `.srm` is a fixed three-slice container. Every slice
+  has 128 KiB plus a canonical 16-byte RTC trailer, so changing the visible
+  count never renames or shifts a player's save. The frontend mirrors mGBA's
+  save-type-specific RTC location (8, 64, or 128 KiB) into that fixed trailer.
+- Version 0.3.0 intentionally has no link cable, subsystems, savestates,
+  user-configurable frameskip, sensors, or background file writes.
+- `Audio output` selects Player 1 (the default), Player 2, or Player 3, or
+  disables audible output. Selecting a player that is not active produces
+  silence rather than changing identity.
+- Each player has an independent speed target from 1.0x through 4.0x in 0.1x
+  increments (default target 2.0x). R2 on that player's controller toggles only
+  that instance between 1.0x and its target. All players start at 1.0x whenever
+  content is loaded.
+
+## FireRed dialogue assist
+
+L2 on each player's controller independently toggles a conservative dialogue
+assist for that instance. `FireRed dialogue speed` selects an integer target
+from 5x through 20x (default 10x). `FireRed dialogue auto-advance` controls
+whether verified page and final waits receive a one-emulated-frame A press;
+every injected press is followed by a forced released frame before acceleration
+can resume. With auto-advance disabled, only verified text printing is sped up.
+
+The detector supports only exact, clean English (USA) Pokemon FireRed ROMs:
+
+- Version 1.0: SHA-1 `41cb23d8dccc8ebd7c649cd8fbb58eeace6e2fdc`
+- Version 1.1: SHA-1 `dd5945db9b930750cb39d00c84da8571feebf417`
+
+The ROM is hashed once at content load. Every other revision, translation,
+ROM hack, and patched image fails closed: L2 reports the feature unavailable,
+the detector does not inspect live game state, and no input is injected.
+
+For supported ROMs, acceleration requires the known overworld callback,
+ordinary field-message printer, and expected script wait to agree. The detector
+also proves that the complete nested return path after the text contains only
+message close/release operations and script termination. It therefore leaves
+story text with side effects or uncertain continuations at 1x. The detector
+blocks on battle startup, in-battle state (including trainer battles and wild
+Pokemon encounters), trainer- and scripted-encounter lead-ins, yes/no prompts,
+multichoice prompts, and every unknown state. It rechecks the state between
+every accelerated emulated frame and returns immediately to normal scheduling
+when any safety condition stops matching.
+
+## Execution model
+
+One-instance mode uses the same direct `runFrame()` path as the stock core.
+For two or three instances, Player 1 remains on libretro's caller thread while
+Players 2 and 3 each have one persistent owner pthread. The caller waits at a
+fixed barrier before it reads video/audio memory, invokes a frontend callback,
+or returns. At normal speed, a generation contains exactly one frame per
+instance. Manual acceleration uses an integer tenths accumulator and loops a
+bounded one to four frames inside the same generation. Dialogue assist uses the
+same synchronous owner lane with a hard maximum of 20 frames per generation;
+the selected multiplier is a target and remains limited by available hardware
+time. Neither mode dispatches extra jobs. Intermediate accelerated frames skip
+software rendering, with output rendered at the generation boundary. There is
+no free-running producer, catch-up loop, deferred input, or work queue that can
+grow over time.
+
+This differs intentionally from mGBA's `mCoreThread`, which is a continuously
+running GUI-oriented controller with its own audio/video synchronization. It
+also differs from Gearboy, SameBoy, and TGB Dual's linked modes: those advance
+instances sequentially or by cycle delta because their serial cables require
+sub-frame lockstep. Independent linkless instances do not have that dependency.
+
+Workers never invoke libretro callbacks or perform file I/O. They only apply a
+copied key mask and execute their bounded frame count (or an explicit reset).
+tvOS chooses the physical A12 cores; the frontend does not use hard CPU
+affinity or force a QoS class. Instances share one immutable ROM backing through
+independent VFile cursors; mGBA's built-in copy-on-write path gives an instance
+private storage before GPIO, patch, or emulated cartridge-memory writes.
+
+## Audio rules
+
+mGBA games may change the internal GBA sample rate through `SOUNDBIAS` after
+boot. The frontend therefore keeps the upstream converter for every instance
+and always advertises one fixed 65,536 Hz stream. A per-instance mAVStream hook
+drains old-rate samples on the instance's owner thread before changing the
+converter rate. Only the selected instance is converted; unselected native
+buffers are cleared without copying. During acceleration, the selected stream
+is resampled by the exact configured speed ratio while the frontend cadence
+remains unchanged. Every accelerated subframe is drained or cleared before the
+next one, so the fixed native rings cannot accumulate data.
+
+Manual, dialogue, and normal-speed transitions are applied on the instance's
+owner lane. Converter/FIFO transition state is reset instead of preserving
+samples at the old ratio, and the one-frame press/release path runs at normal
+speed. Thus a dialogue boundary can produce a short bounded silence, but it
+cannot leave queued high-speed audio or retry debt behind.
+
+The output frame count uses an exact rational accumulator:
+
+`65536 * frameCycles / frequency`
+
+This produces the repeating 1097/1098 cadence without rounding drift. Each
+selected FIFO is fixed at 8192 stereo frames. Missing samples are zero-filled;
+oldest samples are discarded at a bounded lead as well as at the hard capacity.
+Disabled audio still submits the exact-count silent stream, preserving
+RetroArch's audio pacing. The cadence advances even when no frontend callback
+is present, and a partial callback never creates retry debt. Audio is never
+dynamically reallocated or allowed to create backpressure.
+
+## Build
+
+```sh
+cmake -S . -B build-multi \
+  -DBUILD_LIBRETRO_MULTI=ON \
+  -DBUILD_LIBRETRO=OFF \
+  -DM_CORE_GBA=ON \
+  -DM_CORE_GB=OFF \
+  -DBUILD_QT=OFF \
+  -DBUILD_SDL=OFF \
+  -DENABLE_SCRIPTING=OFF \
+  -DENABLE_DEBUGGERS=OFF \
+  -DDISABLE_DEPS=ON \
+  -DSKIP_LIBRARY=ON
+cmake --build build-multi --target mgba_multi_libretro --parallel
+```
+
+The small host-side support and audio tests can be run with:
+
+```sh
+cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror \
+  -Isrc/platform/libretro \
+  tests/libretro_multi_support_test.c \
+  src/platform/libretro/libretro_multi_support.c \
+  -o multi-support-test
+./multi-support-test
+
+cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror \
+  -Isrc/platform/libretro \
+  tests/libretro_audio_test.c \
+  src/platform/libretro/libretro-audio.c \
+  src/platform/libretro/libretro_multi_support.c \
+  -o multi-audio-test
+./multi-audio-test
+
+cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror \
+  -Isrc/platform/libretro \
+  tests/libretro_firered_dialogue_test.c \
+  src/platform/libretro/libretro_firered_dialogue.c \
+  -o firered-dialogue-test
+./firered-dialogue-test
+```
+
+## Hardware acceptance
+
+On Apple TV 4K (2nd generation), compare Parallel and Sequential execution
+with RetroArch Run-Ahead, Preemptive Frames, and Rewind disabled. A release
+passes only when emulated time matches the stock mGBA core, all three controller
+ports remain isolated, memory/audio depth stay flat for 30–60 minutes, and
+parallel execution lowers the multi-instance frame-time tail without thermal
+collapse.
diff --git a/src/platform/libretro/libretro-audio.c b/src/platform/libretro/libretro-audio.c
index 28d7544..a5f1475 100644
--- a/src/platform/libretro/libretro-audio.c
+++ b/src/platform/libretro/libretro-audio.c
@@ -7,6 +7,15 @@
 
 #include <string.h>
 
+static uint64_t _audioGcd(uint64_t a, uint64_t b) {
+	while (b) {
+		uint64_t remainder = a % b;
+		a = b;
+		b = remainder;
+	}
+	return a;
+}
+
 void audioConverterReset(struct LibretroAudioConverter* conv, unsigned inputRate) {
 	conv->inputRate = inputRate;
 	conv->prevLeft = 0;
@@ -17,6 +26,9 @@ void audioConverterReset(struct LibretroAudioConverter* conv, unsigned inputRate
 	conv->accCount = 0;
 	conv->factor = 1;
 	conv->phase = 0;
+	conv->scaledPhase = 0;
+	conv->phaseStep = 0;
+	conv->phaseLimit = 0;
 	switch (inputRate) {
 	case GBA_OUTPUT_RATE / 2:
 		conv->mode = CONV_UPSAMPLE;
@@ -39,6 +51,28 @@ void audioConverterReset(struct LibretroAudioConverter* conv, unsigned inputRate
 	}
 }
 
+void audioConverterResetScaled(struct LibretroAudioConverter* conv, unsigned inputRate, unsigned speedTenths) {
+	if (speedTenths < 10) {
+		speedTenths = 10;
+	} else if (speedTenths > 200) {
+		speedTenths = 200;
+	}
+
+	/* Keep the normal-speed fast paths and their exact sample behavior. */
+	if (speedTenths == 10 || !inputRate) {
+		audioConverterReset(conv, inputRate);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	audioConverterReset(conv, inputRate);
+	conv->mode = CONV_RESAMPLE_SCALED;
+	conv->phaseStep = (uint64_t) inputRate * speedTenths;
+	conv->phaseLimit = (uint64_t) GBA_OUTPUT_RATE * 10;
+	uint64_t divisor = _audioGcd(conv->phaseStep, conv->phaseLimit);
+	conv->phaseStep /= divisor;
+	conv->phaseLimit /= divisor;
+}
+
 size_t audioConverterProcess(struct LibretroAudioConverter* conv, const int16_t* in, size_t nFrames, int16_t* out) {
 	size_t i;
 	size_t outFrames = 0;
@@ -106,5 +140,32 @@ size_t audioConverterProcess(struct LibretroAudioConverter* conv, const int16_t*
 		}
 		return outFrames;
 	}
+	case CONV_RESAMPLE_SCALED: {
+		/* Effective input rate/output rate is stored as a reduced ratio. */
+		size_t outCapacity = nFrames * 2;
+		for (i = 0; i < nFrames; ++i) {
+			int16_t left = in[i * 2];
+			int16_t right = in[i * 2 + 1];
+			if (!conv->havePrev) {
+				conv->prevLeft = left;
+				conv->prevRight = right;
+				conv->havePrev = true;
+			}
+			while (conv->scaledPhase < conv->phaseLimit) {
+				if (outFrames < outCapacity) {
+					int64_t leftDelta = (int64_t) (left - conv->prevLeft) * conv->scaledPhase;
+					int64_t rightDelta = (int64_t) (right - conv->prevRight) * conv->scaledPhase;
+					out[outFrames * 2] = (int16_t) (conv->prevLeft + leftDelta / (int64_t) conv->phaseLimit);
+					out[outFrames * 2 + 1] = (int16_t) (conv->prevRight + rightDelta / (int64_t) conv->phaseLimit);
+					++outFrames;
+				}
+				conv->scaledPhase += conv->phaseStep;
+			}
+			conv->scaledPhase -= conv->phaseLimit;
+			conv->prevLeft = left;
+			conv->prevRight = right;
+		}
+		return outFrames;
+	}
 	}
 }
